WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.933
- [Bim] Hi, I'm Bim Adewunmi.

00:00:00.933 --> 00:00:02.740
- [Ahmed] I'm Ahmed Ali Akbar.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:04.288
- [Bim] And this Ramadan, I'm going to be making

00:00:04.288 --> 00:00:07.780
some Nigerian style potato and egg for suhoor.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.050
- [Ahmed] And I'm gonna be making

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.470
some Pakistani style pakoras for iftar.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.920
- [Bim] Ramadan is a month of the Islamic calendar

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.240
and during that month you fast from sunup to sundown

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.810
every day, and so suhoor is the predawn meal.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.470
You have this before the sun comes up,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.150
and when I was growing up the thing that we had

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.400
most commonly was something that we Nigerians call egg,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.700
singular, always egg, and you can put anything in there,

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.740
like sardines or corned beef or sausages, or whatever,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.553
like it really is a DIY situation.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.231
- [Ahmed] I think eggs are a really common thing.

00:00:36.231 --> 00:00:37.793
They're easy, they're quick.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.120
- [Bim] High protein, super nutritious

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.370
and they fill you up for longer

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.820
so you don't feel the pangs of hunger nearly as much.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.293
- [Ahmed] You're looking at basically 12 to 19 hours

00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:50.250
of not eating or drinking during the day.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.060
- [Bim] And that includes water, before you ask.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.250
- [Ahmed] Fasting means different things to different people

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.601
but it's ultimately a way to try to make yourself

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:58.730
a better person during the month of Ramadan.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.463
Unfortunately, also what happens is sometimes

00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:02.260
when you break your fast you're very hungry

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.210
and you eat a lot of food, so the thing that I make

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.833
is something that I shovel in my face,

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.050
when it's time to break fast.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:10.883
- [Bim] Tell us a little bit about

00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:12.310
what you're gonna make for iftar.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.500
- [Ahmed] So iftar is the time where you break your fast,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.680
you know, you hear the call to prayer,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.703
you eat a date and you drink some water

00:01:17.703 --> 00:01:20.680
and then, somebody's probably already preparing the food,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.140
and one of the things that we always prepare

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.990
is this pakora, which is a fried vegetable fritter.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.010
The pakoras can basically be any fried vegetable.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.830
It's like the chickpea flour is the thing

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.430
that really makes it what it is,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.370
it's usually potatoes, onions, jalapenos, eggplant,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.629
it's really anything you want, so it's like a really

00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:40.900
special treat, this is like the unhealthy side

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.220
of your fast breaking, what I love about pakoras

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.240
is that they're not always pretty,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.870
they're just like a whole bunch of, mess of vegetables

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:48.703
with batter on top of them,

00:01:48.703 --> 00:01:51.590
so I like it when there's a jalapeno and an onion

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.420
and a potato in one pakora.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.720
They are a really special thing of bringing people together

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.890
where everyone wants to pluck them

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.500
right out of the oil when they're hot.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.183
They don't even get to a plate really, you know,

00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:03.390
because they're best when they're immediately

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:04.541
crispy out of the oil.

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:06.958
(soft music)

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:09.860
- [Bim] I love Ramadan, every year.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.300
It makes me feel really connected,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.640
there are literally millions of us across the world

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.160
all doing the same thing at the same time.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.750
I found that really heartwarming.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.080
It makes me incredibly happy, like I'm really part

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.495
of a global community.

00:02:20.495 --> 00:02:21.745
[Ahmed] Oh yes.

00:02:23.171 --> 00:02:25.588
(soft music)

